# Ozituma environment configuration.
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. Never commit real secrets.
#
# WHERE THE FILE GOES, AND WHY IT CATCHES PEOPLE OUT
#
# This file documents every variable the platform uses, but there is no single
# .env.local that serves them all. Next.js loads .env files from THE DIRECTORY IT
# IS RUN IN, the one holding next.config.ts, and it does not walk up to the
# monorepo root. So:
#
#   apps/web/.env.local          the dictionary (ozituma.com)
#   apps/web/.env.local          the dictionary (ozituma.com)
#   (the courses app, `apps/learn`, has been deleted — see AGENTS.md, "What was removed")
#   apps/ozikoro/.env.local      the archive (ozikoro.com)
#   ./.env.local                 root-level scripts only
#
# A value in the root .env.local is INVISIBLE to the apps, and the failure is
# silent: nothing errors, the feature simply behaves as though it were never
# configured. That is exactly how a correctly-filled Spotify credential shows up
# as "Not configured yet" on the admin screen. Put each app's variables in that
# app's directory.
#
# In production none of this applies: the container receives real environment
# variables (see docker/docker-compose.prod.yml), and no .env.local is read.
#
# The .gitignore rule is `.env.local` with no leading slash, so it matches at
# every depth and every one of these files is ignored. Keep it that way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave DATABASE_URL unset for local development: Ozituma then uses PGlite
# (real Postgres compiled to WebAssembly) in .data/pg, with no install needed.
#
# Set it to use a real Postgres — required for Docker, CI with a service
# container, and production (RDS/Aurora). Note that PGlite is single-process,
# so a running dev server and a CLI script cannot share one PGlite directory;
# use this variable when you need both at once.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://ozituma:ozituma@localhost:5432/ozituma

# Where PGlite keeps its data when DATABASE_URL is unset.
# Defaults to <repo>/.data/pg
# OZITUMA_DB_PATH=.data/pg

# Connection pool ceiling per instance.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Log every SQL statement with timings.
# OZITUMA_DB_DEBUG=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Object storage (S3-compatible)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for pronunciation audio and document uploads. Works unchanged against
# AWS S3, MinIO or any S3-compatible service.
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# S3_BUCKET=ozituma-media
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Set true for MinIO and most non-AWS providers; false for AWS S3 virtual-host style.
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Public CDN base for media, if you front the bucket with CloudFront.
# MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for canonical URLs, the OpenAPI server list and sitemap generation.
OZITUMA_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
OZITUMA_VERSION=dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared accounts across ozituma.com and learn.ozituma.com
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A leading-dot parent domain, e.g. .ozituma.com. Setting it makes ONE session cookie valid for
# both the dictionary and the courses, so an account created on either works on both — §6.2's
# "shared auth cookie domain (.ozituma.com)".
#
# Leave it UNSET in development. A browser silently discards a cookie whose domain does not match
# the request, and logs nothing, so a wrong value here looks exactly like a broken sign-in. Unset
# keeps the previous host-only behaviour, which cannot break anything.
#
# It cannot span ozikoro.com: that is a different registrable domain, and no cookie setting crosses
# two registrable domains. That pairing needs SSO. The design record for the arrangement that
# needed this is gone with the courses host; what is left is the setting itself.
# OZITUMA_AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.ozituma.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Academy (academy.ozikoro.com, not yet live)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **`learn.ozituma.com` WAS RETIRED ENTIRE ON 2026-10-04** and `academy.ozikoro.com` replaces it,
# but has no DNS record yet and must not be brought live from this repository. Read this with
# AGENTS.md, "Two hostnames, two applications", before setting either variable.
#
# The two below belonged to the SUBDOMAIN-SERVING arrangement in `apps/web`, which no longer exists:
# the dictionary does not serve course pages on a second hostname, and `OZITUMA_LEARN_HOST` fed a
# `learn-host.ts` that has been deleted. **They are kept documented rather than deleted because the
# academy's own build will need a canonical origin**, and it should take `academy.ozikoro.com` when
# that host has a record in its zone — not before. `ozituma.com/learn` no longer reads either of them.
#
# The canonical origin written into course canonical links and used for outbound sharing.
# OZITUMA_LEARN_URL=https://academy.ozikoro.com

# The single hostname the courses accept, in production only.
# OZITUMA_LEARN_HOST=academy.ozikoro.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Testing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ozituma.com/learn` does not redirect to a subdomain: it serves the "Academy is being prepared"
# page, so there is no subdomain window to open against the dictionary. **There is no course app in
# this repository to test against any more** — the TanStack Start app in `learn/` was deleted with the
# host it served, so the subdomain tests have nothing to point at.
#
# NOTE: PGlite is single-process (see the Database section above). While
# `npm run dev` is running it holds .data/pg, so a CLI script that calls getDb()
# at the same time will corrupt the directory and the server will start failing
# with "Aborted()". Stop the dev server before running import or test scripts, or
# set DATABASE_URL to a real Postgres and run both at once.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Corpus import
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Directory holding the raw Igbo corpus files. Defaults to
# data/sources/igbo-api. These files are gitignored; see data/sources/README.md.
# OZITUMA_IGBO_SOURCE=./data/sources/igbo-api

# Import only the first N headwords (fast smoke test).
# OZITUMA_IMPORT_LIMIT=200

# Hugging Face token, needed to download the gated CC-BY-4.0 audio corpora.
# HF_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Analytics (PostHog) — §7, §F12, §10.1
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The project token. It is a PUBLISHABLE key (phc_…): PostHog expects it to be readable by
# whoever sends events, and it grants only the ability to write to one project. It is left blank
# here so that a fork does not quietly send its events into Ozituma's project; the real value
# lives in .env.local, which is gitignored.
#
# Do NOT put a PostHog PERSONAL API key (phx_…) in this file. That one can read and delete data
# and is a genuine secret.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# Consent gate. NOTHING is sent to PostHog unless this is true, and it defaults to false.
#
# §13 requires a lawful basis for analytics and a consent banner where one is required. Until the
# learner-facing consent UI exists (F12/F13), this deployment-level flag is the interim: the safe
# state is the default state, and enabling capture is a deliberate act.
# OZITUMA_ANALYTICS_CONSENT=false

# Analytics is captured SERVER-SIDE, with no client SDK. See apps/web/lib/analytics.ts for why:
# every event §F12 asks for is a server-side fact, and routing them through the server means the
# property allowlist in packages/core/src/analytics.ts cannot be bypassed from a browser.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spotify (the ozikoro.com connection)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The connection Ozikoro publishes from, used by apps/ozikoro. The production
# OAuth callback registered with the Spotify Developer Dashboard is:
#
#     https://ozikoro.com/api/spotify/callback
#
# See docs/SPOTIFY.md for the full setup, and for what this integration
# deliberately does not do yet (Spotify's authorisation grants no podcast
# audio-upload capability, so the publishing mechanism is a separate decision).

# From the Spotify Developer Dashboard app.
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=

# Server-side ONLY. This value must never reach frontend code, a client bundle,
# a URL, a log or a Git commit. It is sent to Spotify in an Authorization header
# and nowhere else.
# SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Where Spotify sends the administrator back.
#
# Defaults to https://ozikoro.com/api/spotify/callback, so PRODUCTION NEEDS NO
# CONFIGURATION AT ALL. That is deliberate: the address the Dashboard is
# configured with is the one that works with nothing set, and development and
# staging override it without touching production.
#
# Every value used here must ALSO be added to the Dashboard's redirect URI
# allow-list, and must match it character for character. A mismatch is the most
# common failure and Spotify reports it as an invalid redirect URI.
#
# Plain HTTP is refused in production. Outside production, plain HTTP is allowed
# only on a loopback host, so the whole flow can be run against localhost:
# SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3100/api/spotify/callback

# The key that encrypts the stored Spotify tokens, 32 bytes as 64 hex characters
# or as base64. Generate one with:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Why it exists: a refresh token cannot be hashed, because it has to be presented
# back to Spotify, so it must be recoverable. It is therefore stored as
# AES-256-GCM ciphertext under this key, which lives only in the environment and
# never in the database, so a database dump on its own yields nothing usable.
#
# If it is absent the integration FAILS CLOSED: it refuses to start a connection
# rather than storing a token unencrypted. A value that is not exactly 32 bytes
# is refused rather than stretched, because silently treating a short passphrase
# as a 256-bit key looks like encryption while providing very little of it.
#
# Losing this key is recoverable but not invisible: stored tokens can no longer
# be read, and the administrator reconnects. Changing it without reconnecting has
# the same effect, which is why it is worth keeping somewhere durable.
# SPOTIFY_TOKEN_KEY=

# The scopes requested. Defaults to "user-read-private user-read-email", which is
# what lets the admin screen name the connected account and nothing more.
#
# Left as the default on purpose. A consent screen asking for library or playback
# permissions to power a feature that does not exist yet is how an integration
# loses an administrator's trust. Widen this when a real publishing mechanism
# needs it, not before.
#
# If any entry is malformed the whole list falls back to the default rather than
# keeping the valid entries, so a mistyped scope cannot silently change the grant.
# SPOTIFY_SCOPES=user-read-private user-read-email

# The archive — ozikoro.com
#
# Consumed by the Caddyfile, not by the app: the app's canonical URLs are absolute and built from the
# site URL it knows. www. is redirected to the apex for this domain exactly as for the dictionary.
OZIKORO_DOMAIN=ozikoro.com
OZIKORO_SITE_URL=https://ozikoro.com

# Operations. The backup service is pg_dump against the same postgres — see the note in the compose
# file for why the local backup module cannot do this job. The token enables /api/health's detailed
# branch; without it the endpoint answers the plain check.
HEALTH_TOKEN=

# NOWPayments — crypto donations on the archive
#
# THE IPN SECRET IS WHAT MAKES /api/webhooks/nowpayments TRUSTWORTHY. With it unset the endpoint answers
# 503 and records nothing, deliberately: an endpoint that accepts unsigned "payment finished" calls when
# a key is missing is worse than one that does not exist.
#
# Signature scheme differs from Paystack and the difference is load-bearing: NOWPayments signs the body
# RE-SERIALISED WITH ITS KEYS SORTED, not the raw bytes. So the route parses before it verifies, and a
# body that will not parse is refused with 401 rather than acknowledged.
#
# The API key is used only for the independent confirmation step — the webhook re-checks a payment
# against the API and lets that answer decide, because a signed event may still say "waiting".
NOWPAYMENTS_IPN_SECRET=
NOWPAYMENTS_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SPOKEN RECORDS — an article read aloud, reviewed, then published as a podcast
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Spotify ingests an RSS FEED, and that feed is served from this site at /podcast/feed.xml. **No podcast host
# is required**, and nothing about Spotify's requirements needs one — a host adds analytics and an upload
# endpoint, not access. A host can be pointed at this feed later if wanted.
#
# NOTHING IS PUBLISHED WITHOUT A PERSON APPROVING IT. An episode is rendered into `ozikoro_episode` with the
# status `pending_review`, and it stays out of the feed until somebody listens and approves it. **The whole
# point of the review step is that a mistake is caught before Spotify has it.**
#
# THE VOICE. A clone is created in the owner's own ElevenLabs account and its ID is set here. **A cloned voice
# belongs to the person who trained it** — that is Spotify's second rule, and the reason this takes an ID rather
# than shipping one.
#
#   ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_OWN       a voice cloned from the owner's own recording (narrator_kind
#                                 `synthetic_own_voice`, disclosed as such)
#   ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_GENERIC   a stock narrator, for anything the owner has not read (disclosed as synthetic)
#
# The script is the ARTICLE'S OWN SENTENCES with markup and citation brackets removed. **It is not rewritten.**
# An audiobook-styled dramatisation would assert a tone the record does not have, and a listener could not tell
# which sentences were the archive's.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_OWN=
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID_GENERIC=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BACKUPS — the dump leaves the machine encrypted, or it is not a backup (rounds 353 and 360)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE DATABASE AND ITS DUMPS ARE ON ONE 40 GB DISK. `postgres` binds /opt/ozituma/pgdata and `backup`
# mounts the named volume `backupdata`, and `findmnt` puts both on /dev/nvme0n1p1 — one filesystem, one
# disk. So the dumps must be uploaded, and `backup-upload` does it through the same Cloudflare R2
# credential the applications already use for media.
#
# THESE SIX NAMES ARE FOR THE HOST, NOT FOR DEVELOPMENT. The four below the key have working defaults in
# docker/docker-compose.prod.yml and are listed here so an operator can name them deliberately. **The
# two key names have NO default on purpose, and `scripts/check-compose-env.mjs` reads the container's
# shell entrypoint and reports by NAME**, so a name added here and not to the service block is caught by
# that script rather than showing up as a service that starts cleanly and does nothing.
#
# ── THE KEY. READ THIS BEFORE YOU GENERATE ONE. ──────────────────────────────
#
# The dump is encrypted with `age` before it is written to the volume. `age` has no recovery path and
# no back door: **if the identity is lost, every dump it encrypted is unreadable forever.** Not
# recoverable by Cloudflare, not by AWS, not by a later round of this project. An encrypted backup with
# no key is not a backup; it is a file.
#
# Generate both halves ON THE HOST, in one command, and store the output in the Secrets Manager secret
# that writes this file at boot (see infrastructure/ozituma-stack.yaml — the `APP_SECRET` JSON is
# written into /opt/ozituma/.env on every boot, so a key put only in .env is one rebuilt host away from
# being gone). `age-keygen` is in the backup image, so there is nothing to install:
#
#   cd /opt/ozituma/app
#   docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm -T --entrypoint age-keygen backup
#
# It prints the public key on a `# public key:` line and the identity on the next. Copy the public key
# into BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT and the identity into BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY — **and never the other way
# round**, because a recipient pasted into the identity variable is a private key written into a
# transcript. Then keep a THIRD copy off the box — printed, or in the password manager, or in a sealed
# envelope. The numbered procedure, including where each copy goes and how to check them, is §4a of
# docs/OZIKORO-CUTOVER.md. Do not skip the third copy.
#
#   BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT            the PUBLIC half, `age1…`. Not a secret: it is the address a dump is
#                                   encrypted TO. A wrong one means every dump is encrypted to a key
#                                   nobody holds, so the dumper checks the pair at start-up and refuses.
#   BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY             the PRIVATE half, `AGE-SECRET-KEY-1…`. **This is the key.** It is read
#                                   by BOTH services: the dumper to verify what it just wrote by
#                                   decrypting it, the uploader to decrypt before it uploads and again
#                                   on the object it reads back out of R2.
#
# ── THE PIPELINE'S OWN NAMES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   BACKUP_UPLOAD_PREFIX            default `backups/ozituma`. REFUSED at start-up unless it begins
#                                   `backups/`, because the bucket also holds `ozikoro/` (the archive's
#                                   media) and `audio/` (72,015 recordings), and a mis-prefixed backup
#                                   that overwrote a media object would be the worst outcome of the job.
#   BACKUP_LOCAL_RETENTION_DAYS     default 14. A local dump is deleted only when it is older than this
#                                   AND its object is confirmed in R2. A dump that has not uploaded is
#                                   never deleted, however old it is.
#   BACKUP_REMOTE_RETENTION_DAYS    default 45. Older than the local number on purpose, so there is always
#                                   a window in which the same dump exists in both places.
#   BACKUP_UPLOAD_INTERVAL_SECONDS  default 60.
#
# THE CREDENTIAL IS THE EXISTING ONE and is not shown here: `S3_ENDPOINT`, `S3_BUCKET`, `S3_REGION`,
# `S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE`, `S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` above already carry it, and the
# uploader is given the same names. It needs PutObject, GetObject, HeadObject, ListBucket and — for
# remote retention only — DeleteObject. Nothing new is granted for the upload itself; the delete is the
# one operation this adds, and it is scoped in code to BACKUP_UPLOAD_PREFIX.
#
# WHY THE ENCRYPTION WAS NOT OPTIONAL. `media.ozituma.com` is this bucket's custom domain and it serves
# objects ANONYMOUSLY — measured with no credentials at all:
#
#   curl -sI https://media.ozituma.com/ozikoro/9274-osm-intl8aa250x200@2x.png
#     -> HTTP/2 200, content-type: image/png, content-length: 47361
#
# while the R2 endpoint's own door refuses the same object (400). A dump holds every account row, every
# session, every password hash and every audit entry, and `backups/ozituma/ozituma-<timestamp>.dump` was
# a guessable key. It is now `ozituma-<timestamp>.dump.age`, and a guessed key yields ciphertext. That is
# the whole fix: the public front door no longer matters, because the bytes behind it are useless without
# the identity above.
#
# THE VALUES BELOW ARE PLACEHOLDERS AND MUST NEVER BE REAL. The identity shown is deliberately not a key.
#
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...replace-me-with-age-keygen-output...
# BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY=AGE-SECRET-KEY-1...replace-me-with-age-keygen-output...
# BACKUP_UPLOAD_PREFIX=backups/ozituma
# BACKUP_LOCAL_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# BACKUP_REMOTE_RETENTION_DAYS=45
# BACKUP_UPLOAD_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
